\begin{abstract}
Coronary atherosclerotic plaque rupture is a major cause of acute coronary events, and its risk is governed jointly by plaque morphology, tissue material properties, and hemodynamic loading. Which of these factor groups most strongly governs rupture-risk variability remains poorly characterized, partly because pulsatile fluid--structure interaction (FSI) is computationally expensive. Here, a cost-effective snapshot-based FSI formulation was used as the forward model to generate large input--output datasets for low-attenuation (LAP) and calcified (CP) plaque phenotypes, sampled by Latin hypercube design over morphological, material, and hemodynamic parameters. Two stress-based vulnerability indices, the peak plaque structural stress (PSS) and its pulsatile amplitude ($\Delta$PSS), were evaluated and first checked for directional consistency with five clinically recognized high-risk plaque features. Gaussian process regression surrogates were then trained for each index, and Sobol variance-based global sensitivity analysis was performed at the individual-variable and factor-group levels. $\Delta$PSS was consistent with all five high-risk features, whereas PSS was consistent with three, indicating that the pulsatile stress amplitude is the more clinically consistent index. The surrogates were accurate ($R^2 = 0.94$--$0.97$). Across both phenotypes and indices, the vessel-wall modulus, fibrous-cap modulus, and systolic pressure were the dominant contributors, and the material group showed the largest total-order contribution ($S_T = 0.596$--$0.708$), followed by the hemodynamic group, whereas morphology contributed mainly through interactions. These results indicate that coronary plaque rupture-risk variability is governed primarily by material and hemodynamic factors rather than by morphology alone, providing a basis for prioritizing the quantities that warrant accurate patient-specific characterization.
\end{abstract}

\begin{highlights}
\item Morphological, material, and hemodynamic factors varied jointly over a large FSI set
\item Two stress indices, PSS and $\Delta$PSS, assessed against clinical high-risk features
\item $\Delta$PSS is more clinically consistent than peak systolic stress (PSS)
\item Sobol GSA ranks material properties as the dominant factor group for rupture risk
\end{highlights}

