\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Cardiovascular disease (CVD) remains the leading cause of death worldwide, and ischemic heart disease alone accounted for approximately 9.0 million deaths in 2021~\cite{GBD2021}. Atherosclerotic plaque rupture is a major mechanism underlying acute coronary events. Rupture of the fibrous cap exposes thrombogenic plaque contents to the bloodstream and can precipitate coronary thrombosis and myocardial infarction~\cite{bentzon2014, costopoulos2017}. From a mechanical perspective, rupture occurs when the local stress acting on the fibrous cap exceeds its mechanical strength~\cite{costopoulos2017}. The mechanical loading of the fibrous cap is therefore central to plaque stability and rupture risk.

Advances in coronary computed tomography angiography and intravascular imaging have enabled increasingly detailed \textit{in vivo} characterization of plaque geometry and composition~\cite{lu2021highrisk, williams2020lap, milzi2021}. These developments have also supported image-based computational models that estimate local mechanical quantities not directly available from imaging alone. Among these quantities, plaque structural stress (PSS) is widely used to characterize the mechanical loading associated with plaque rupture~\cite{teng2014, costopoulos2017}. The local stress field depends on plaque geometry, tissue material properties, and the applied physiological loading~\cite{huang2001, costopoulos2017, stefanati2024}. Clinical imaging studies have further associated elevated PSS with plaque composition, phenotype, and rupture~\cite{teng2014, costopoulos2017, milzi2021}. PSS is commonly evaluated at or near systole to represent peak plaque loading~\cite{teng2014, costopoulos2017}. Other studies have examined the pulsatile variation in structural stress ($\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$), motivated by repeated cyclic loading and potential fatigue-related damage accumulation~\cite{bank2000, costopoulos2017}. The present study therefore considers both PSS and $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$ as complementary stress-based measures of plaque vulnerability.

However, estimating a vulnerability index for a given lesion does not identify which underlying inputs drive variation in that index across the plausible parameter space. This distinction is important because plaque morphology, tissue properties, and hemodynamic conditions all vary between patients. Moreover, these quantities cannot be measured or prescribed with equal accuracy in patient-specific models. Identifying the dominant contributors can therefore help prioritize measurement, calibration, and model-refinement efforts. Global sensitivity analysis (GSA) provides a systematic framework for this purpose by quantifying how uncertain inputs contribute to output variability over their prescribed ranges~\cite{saltelli2008}. Variance-based GSA can also capture interaction effects that are not resolved when factors are examined only in isolation~\cite{saltelli2008}. Such interactions are particularly relevant to plaque mechanics because the effect of a geometric feature may depend on the surrounding material and hemodynamic state.

Despite this need, previous computational studies of plaque mechanics have generally examined restricted subsets of the relevant factor space. Parametric studies have investigated selected geometric and mechanical factors within predefined model settings~\cite{cilla2012, polzer2019}. Patient-specific studies have related plaque morphology and composition to structural stress~\cite{teng2014, costopoulos2017, milzi2021}, while other work has examined the effect of variability in tissue mechanical properties~\cite{stefanati2024}. These studies have provided important insight into individual determinants of plaque stress. However, they do not provide a common variance decomposition across morphological, material, and hemodynamic factor groups. Restricted parametric designs also provide limited information on interactions among these groups. A further limitation is computational scale. Pulsatile fluid--structure interaction (FSI) couples time-dependent blood flow with deformable vessel and plaque mechanics and is computationally expensive~\cite{wang2021_multipatient, lissoni2025_coronary_methods}. This cost complicates the large number of model evaluations required for variance-based GSA, for which stable sensitivity estimates depend on adequate sampling of the input space~\cite{saltelli2008}. The relative contributions of morphology, material properties, and hemodynamic loading therefore remain insufficiently characterized within a unified large-scale analysis.

Accordingly, this study aimed to determine which of the morphological, material, and hemodynamic factor groups most strongly governs the variability of coronary plaque rupture risk. A cost-effective snapshot-based FSI formulation was used as the forward model to generate large computational datasets for low-attenuation and calcified plaque phenotypes. Two stress-based vulnerability indices, PSS and $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$, were evaluated. Their directional consistency with clinically recognized high-risk plaque features was first assessed before sensitivity interpretation. Gaussian process regression surrogates were then trained for each index, followed by Sobol variance-based GSA at both the individual-variable and factor-group levels. This framework quantifies direct and total contributions, including interaction-related effects, across broad physiologically plausible input ranges. The analysis was designed to identify the dominant determinants of plaque-vulnerability variability and to provide a mechanistic basis for prioritizing quantities that warrant accurate characterization in future patient-specific assessment.

