\section{Methods}
\label{sec:methods}

The proposed framework consists of three stages (Fig.~\ref{fig:framework}). First, a snapshot-based fluid--structure interaction (FSI) solver was used as the forward model to generate large input--output datasets for two coronary plaque phenotypes (low-attenuation and calcified). Second, two vulnerability indices (VIs; PSS and $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$) were checked for consistency with clinically recognized high-risk features. Third, a Gaussian process regression (GPR) surrogate was trained for each VI, and Sobol variance-based global sensitivity analysis (GSA) was performed to quantify the contribution of each factor group to the variability of rupture risk.

\subsection{Snapshot-based FSI forward solver}
The forward model employed in this study is the snapshot-based pulsatile FSI solver developed and validated in a previous study~\cite{TODO_kim_snapshot}. It decouples the temporal and spatial components of the pulsatile load. A single one-dimensional (1D) reduced-order hemodynamic simulation provides the time-dependent pressure and flow-rate boundary conditions, which are then imposed on steady three-dimensional (3D) fluid and quasi-static structural analyses at selected time instants to reconstruct the instantaneous vessel-wall stress field.

In this forward solver, the 1D reduced-order model (ROM) fluid simulations were performed using an in-house C++ solver on a desktop workstation with an Intel Core i7-12700K processor (3.61~GHz) and 64~GB of RAM. The 3D fluid and solid simulations were conducted on an HPC cluster equipped with Intel Xeon processors (2 sockets, 96 cores) and 257~GB of RAM. The steady 3D fluid analyses were performed using svSolver~\cite{updegrove2017simvascular}, and the quasi-static structural analyses were performed using ANSYS Mechanical APDL (release 2025; ANSYS Inc., Canonsburg, PA, USA).

\subsection{Computational model and input space}
\label{subsec:model}
In this study, two plaque phenotypes commonly identified by coronary computed tomography angiography (CCTA), a widely used non-invasive imaging method, were modeled: low-attenuation plaque (LAP) and calcified plaque (CP)~\cite{lu2021highrisk, williams2020lap}. These were constructed as idealized geometric models (Fig.~\ref{fig:geometry}A). The LAP was defined as a three-domain model comprising the vessel wall, the fibrous cap (FC), and the lipid core, whereas the CP was defined as a four-domain model with an additional calcification domain inside the lipid core. The input parameters were organized into three groups---morphological, hemodynamic, and material---whose definitions, ranges, and sources are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:inputs}.

\subsubsection{Geometry and morphology parameters}
The baseline coronary geometry was modeled as a cylindrical segment with a length of $10\,\mathrm{cm}$, a lumen normal diameter of $d_\mathrm{ref} = 0.2\,\mathrm{cm}$, and a wall thickness of $t_\mathrm{wall} = 0.02\,\mathrm{cm}$~\cite{dodge1992lumen_r_av, fung1993biomechanics, caro2012mechanics}. Since fibrous-cap thickness can only be resolved by invasive imaging such as optical coherence tomography (OCT)~\cite{jang2005oct, kume2005octimt}, it was fixed at a uniform representative value of $t_\mathrm{FC} = 100\,\mu\mathrm{m}$ for all baseline samples.

The lesion morphology was parameterized by six variables common to both phenotypes (Table~\ref{tab:inputs}): degree of stenosis ($DoS$), lesion length ($L_\mathrm{lesion}$), lumen axial skewness ($\gamma_z$), positive remodeling index ($PI$), lipid arc angle ($\theta_\mathrm{lipid,max}$), and lipid length ratio ($l_\mathrm{lipid}$). These parameters determine the shape of the lumen, fibrous cap, lipid core and vessel wall. To reproduce the irregular morphology of plaque calcifications, the calcification was generated inside the lipid core using a seed-based region-growing algorithm. Its total volume was set to $f_\mathrm{calc} \cdot V_\mathrm{lipid}$, while four auxiliary shape parameters controlled the seed location and growth direction within the lipid core. These auxiliary parameters were excluded from the surrogate training and sensitivity analysis because an initial Sobol screening confirmed that their contribution to the output was negligible (all first-order indices $S_1 < 10^{-3}$). Moreover, they are numerical controls for shape generation rather than clinically interpretable variables.

Based on these morphology parameters, a CAD model was generated in Autodesk Inventor Professional 2026 (Autodesk Inc., San Francisco, CA, USA). The solid domains were meshed in Gmsh~\cite{geuzaine2009gmsh} with approximately $3\times10^{6}$ second-order tetrahedral elements, and the fluid domain in Simmetrix MeshSim (Simmetrix Inc., Clifton Park, NY, USA) with approximately $5\times10^{6}$ first-order tetrahedral elements and three prism boundary layers along the lumen wall.

