\subsubsection{Boundary conditions and hemodynamic parameters}
For the fluid inlet boundary condition, a physiological pressure waveform $P_\mathrm{in}(t)$ was prescribed. This waveform was generated by coupling a fixed reference coronary flow waveform $Q(t)$ with a three-element Windkessel (RCR) model, fixing the coronary resistance ratio at $R_p/R_d = 1/10$ and fitting the remaining parameters by least squares to the target $(P_\mathrm{sys},\ P_\mathrm{dia} = P_\mathrm{sys} - \Delta P)$~\cite{westerhof2009windkessel}. Thus, $P_\mathrm{in}(t)$ for each sample is fully determined by the three hemodynamic parameters: systolic pressure $P_\mathrm{sys}$, pulse pressure $\Delta P$, and diastolic decay ratio $\tau$ (Table~\ref{tab:inputs}). For the outlet boundary condition, a lumped-parameter coronary model was imposed at each outlet to represent the distal coronary microvasculature, with a time-varying intramyocardial pressure accounting for myocardial compression~\cite{kim2010coronary}. Its parameters were calibrated to reference coronary flow and pressure values~\cite{guyton2011_av_P, Sankaran2012}. For the solid boundary condition, the wall pressure field reconstructed from the steady 3D fluid simulation was applied as a surface traction on the inner wall of the multi-domain solid mesh, and both ends of the segment were constrained as fixed ends. The fluid and solid boundary conditions are summarized in Fig.~\ref{fig:bc}.

\subsubsection{Material property parameters}
Although arterial tissue exhibits anisotropic and hyperelastic behavior, the coronary domains undergo only small deformations over the cardiac cycle and were therefore modeled as isotropic linear elastic materials~\cite{milzi2021, loree1992}. The Young's modulus of each solid domain was treated as an input parameter (Table~\ref{tab:inputs}). The Poisson's ratio was set to $\nu = 0.49$ for the vessel wall, fibrous cap, and lipid core to approximate nearly incompressible soft vascular/plaque tissues, whereas the calcification was assigned $\nu = 0.30$~\cite{cheng1993distribution, huang2001impact, milzi2021coronary}. Blood in the fluid domain was modeled as an incompressible Newtonian fluid with a density of $1.06\,\mathrm{g/cm^3}$ and a dynamic viscosity of $\mu = 0.04\,\mathrm{poise}$~\cite{ku1997blood}.

\subsubsection{Input parameter sampling}
The range of each input parameter was determined from previous clinical and imaging studies and is summarized, together with its definition, in Table~\ref{tab:inputs}. Each variable was assumed to follow an independent uniform distribution within its range, and 1,000 input vectors were generated per phenotype by Latin hypercube sampling (LHS)~\cite{mckay1979lhs}. The LAP was sampled over its 12 analysis variables, and the CP was sampled over an 18-dimensional design comprising the 14 analysis variables (the 12 common variables plus $f_\mathrm{calc}$ and $E_\mathrm{calc}$) and four auxiliary parameters controlling the calcification-shape generation. During sampling, the constraint $P_\mathrm{dia} > 40\,\mathrm{mmHg}$ was imposed to exclude physiologically unrealistic low-pressure conditions~\cite{heward2025cpp, mcevoy2016diastolic}. After excluding samples that failed during geometry generation, Boolean operations, or meshing, $784/1{,}000$ and $756/1{,}000$ valid input--output pairs remained for the LAP and CP, respectively. In addition, a separate auxiliary dataset was generated to verify that the forward solver reproduces the clinically recognized inverse relationship between fibrous-cap thickness and cap stress. In this set, $t_\mathrm{FC}$ was varied from $40$ to $200\,\mu\mathrm{m}$ in $40\,\mu\mathrm{m}$ increments and $E_\mathrm{FC}$ over $1.2$, $1.6$, and $2.0\,\mathrm{MPa}$, with all other inputs fixed at the midpoints of their prescribed ranges.

\subsection{Output quantities}
\subsubsection{Vulnerability indices (PSS and $\Delta$PSS)}
For each sample, end-systole ($t_\mathrm{sys}$) and end-diastole ($t_\mathrm{dia}$) were identified from the inlet pressure waveform, and the forward solver was evaluated at both instants. This yielded the nodal maximum-principal stress fields on the fibrous-cap surface, denoted as $\sigma_n(t_\mathrm{sys})$ and $\sigma_n(t_\mathrm{dia})$ for each fibrous-cap surface node $n$. The plaque structural stress (PSS) was defined as the largest end-systolic maximum-principal stress over all fibrous-cap surface nodes. The pulsatile stress amplitude ($\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$) was defined as the largest absolute systolic--diastolic change in maximum-principal stress over those nodes~\cite{costopoulos2017, TODO_new_brown_prospect}:
\begin{align}
\mathrm{PSS} &= \max_{n \in \Gamma_{FC}} \sigma_n(t_\mathrm{sys}), \\
\Delta\mathrm{PSS} &= \max_{n \in \Gamma_{FC}} \big|\sigma_n(t_\mathrm{sys}) - \sigma_n(t_\mathrm{dia})\big|,
\end{align}
where $\Gamma_{FC}$ is the set of fibrous-cap surface nodes. To suppress single-node noise, $\sigma_n$ at each node was spatially averaged within a spherical neighborhood of radius $r = 0.01\,\mathrm{cm}$ before the maximum was taken~\cite{corti2022}.

Rupture occurs when the local stress exceeds the fibrous-cap strength, so the vulnerability of a plaque is governed by the stress-to-strength ratio~\cite{corti2022}. However, because the absolute fibrous-cap strength is highly uncertain and cannot be prescribed on a sample-specific basis, it was treated as a constant in this study. Under this assumption, each vulnerability index is proportional to its corresponding stress measure, and PSS and $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$ were therefore adopted directly as the two vulnerability indices (VIs).

\subsubsection{Rupture-location analysis}
For each sample, the locations of the maximum PSS and maximum $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$ were localized axially and circumferentially (Fig.~\ref{fig:ruploc}). Axially, each location was classified as proximal, mid, or distal using the lumen area ratio $LA(z) = A_\mathrm{lumen}(z)/A_\mathrm{normal}$, with the mid region bounded by the two locations where $LA = 0.3$, following Chung et al.~\cite{chung2017_LA}. Circumferentially, the angle $\theta_\mathrm{rupture}$ from the reference $y$-axis to the rupture node, measured on the rupture plane, was normalized by the lipid arc angle $\theta_\mathrm{lipid}$ on the same cross-section:
\begin{align}
I_\theta = \frac{|\theta_\mathrm{rupture}|}{\theta_\mathrm{lipid}}.
\end{align}
Each rupture was then classified as center ($I_\theta < 0.5$) or shoulder ($I_\theta \geq 0.5$), where the threshold of $0.5$ corresponds to the geometric midpoint between the cap center and the plaque shoulder over the lipid arc.

\subsection{Clinical consistency of the vulnerability indices}
\label{subsec:consistency}
This stage examines whether the two vulnerability indices, PSS and $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$, behave consistently with clinically recognized markers of plaque vulnerability. Five clinically recognized high-risk plaque features were used for this check. Each feature was represented by a model-measurable quantity, and the expected sign of the correlation between that quantity and each VI was defined according to its association with plaque vulnerability. For example, the high-risk feature ``soft plaque'' was represented by the lipid-core modulus $E_\mathrm{lipid}$, for which a negative correlation with each VI was expected because a softer lipid core is associated with greater plaque vulnerability.

Three of the five features were represented directly by baseline input variables ($E_\mathrm{lipid}$, $f_\mathrm{calc}$, and $\theta_\mathrm{lipid}$). The remaining two, plaque burden ($PB$) and functional gradient ($\Delta\mathrm{FFR}$), were not input variables but were instead derived from the geometry and simulation results. Plaque burden was evaluated at the minimum-lumen-area (MLA) cross-section, defined as the plane at which the lumen diameter was smallest. It was computed in the conventional cross-sectional form as $PB = (A_\mathrm{vessel} - A_\mathrm{lumen})/A_\mathrm{vessel} \times 100\%$, where $A_\mathrm{vessel}$ and $A_\mathrm{lumen}$ are the vessel-wall-bounded and luminal areas on that plane~\cite{mintz2001ivus}. $\Delta\mathrm{FFR}$ was computed as $FFR_\mathrm{prox} - FFR_\mathrm{dist} = 1 - P_d/P_\mathrm{in}$, with the distal pressure $P_d$ taken as the cross-sectional mean $3\,\mathrm{cm}$ downstream of the lesion~\cite{lee2019emerald, cami2018ffrct}.

For each VI and each representative model quantity, both the Pearson linear correlation coefficient ($r$) and the Spearman rank correlation coefficient ($\rho$) were computed. Because the check was based on the direction of association, the sign of $\rho$ was used and compared with the expected correlation sign defined in Table~\ref{tab:features}. A VI was regarded as clinically consistent for a given feature when the sign of $\rho$ matched the expected sign.

