\subsection{Surrogate-based global sensitivity analysis}
Surrogate-based global sensitivity analysis (GSA) was performed on both vulnerability indices (PSS and $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$). Sobol analysis requires more than $10^4$ model evaluations per phenotype, which is impractical even with the cost-effective FSI forward solver. A Gaussian process regression (GPR) surrogate was therefore trained on the valid input--VI pairs, and the Sobol indices were estimated on the surrogate.

\subsubsection{Gaussian process regression surrogate}
For each VI and phenotype, an individual GPR surrogate was trained to map the input vector ($d = 12$ for the LAP, $d = 14$ for the CP) to a scalar VI, using the valid samples ($784$ for the LAP, $756$ for the CP) with an $8{:}2$ training--test split. The model used a squared-exponential kernel with automatic relevance determination (a separate length scale per input dimension), and the hyperparameters were determined by maximizing the marginal log-likelihood using scikit-learn~\cite{pedregosa2011scikit, rasmussen2006gpml}. Predictive accuracy was reported as the coefficient of determination ($R^2$) on the held-out test set.

\subsubsection{Sobol variance-based indices}
Sobol variance-based GSA was performed on the trained surrogate~\cite{sobol2001global, saltelli2002sobol, saltelli2010variance}. The first-order index $S_i$ (the individual contribution of $X_i$ to the output variance) and the total-order index $S_{Ti}$ (its total contribution including interactions) were estimated using Saltelli sampling based on Sobol quasi-random sequences (SALib). With a base sample size of $N = 1{,}024$, this required $N(d+2)$ surrogate evaluations, i.e., $14{,}336$ for the LAP ($d = 12$) and $16{,}384$ for the CP ($d = 14$). In addition to the individual-variable indices, group-level indices $S_G$ and $S_{TG}$ were computed for the three factor groups $G \in \{\text{morphological, material, hemodynamic}\}$ by treating each group as a grouped input, thereby capturing within-group interactions rather than summing individual indices~\cite{saltelli2008}:
\begin{align}
S_i &= \frac{\operatorname{Var}_{X_i}\!\big[\mathbb{E}_{X_{\sim i}}(Y \mid X_i)\big]}{\operatorname{Var}(Y)}, &
S_{Ti} &= \frac{\mathbb{E}_{X_{\sim i}}\!\big[\operatorname{Var}_{X_i}(Y \mid X_{\sim i})\big]}{\operatorname{Var}(Y)}, \\
S_G &= \frac{\operatorname{Var}_{X_G}\!\big[\mathbb{E}_{X_{\sim G}}(Y \mid X_G)\big]}{\operatorname{Var}(Y)}, &
S_{TG} &= \frac{\mathbb{E}_{X_{\sim G}}\!\big[\operatorname{Var}_{X_G}(Y \mid X_{\sim G})\big]}{\operatorname{Var}(Y)}.
\end{align}

