\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

This section presents the main results for the vulnerability-index consistency check and the subsequent sensitivity analysis. First, the simulation outputs were summarized to characterize the range and distribution of the computational outputs. Next, the two vulnerability indices (PSS and $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$) were checked for whether the sign of their correlation with each model quantity was consistent with the expected high-risk direction. Finally, for both indices, the GPR surrogate accuracy and Sobol global sensitivity indices were reported.

\subsection{Simulation outputs and forward-solver validation}
The simulated PSS values ranged from 43 to 247~kPa, with a median of 101~kPa and an interquartile range of 84--121~kPa. The simulated $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$ values ranged from 14 to 79~kPa, with a median of 31~kPa and an interquartile range of 25--38~kPa (Fig.~\ref{fig:outputs}A). Predicted high-risk locations were then classified in the circumferential and axial directions. Circumferentially, high-risk locations were more frequently identified at the shoulder than at the center for both the PSS-based VI (56.9\% vs.\ 43.1\%) and the $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$-based VI (57.5\% vs.\ 42.5\%) (Fig.~\ref{fig:outputs}B). Axially, high-risk locations were most frequently identified in the middle region near the minimum lumen area, accounting for 60.2\% of cases for the PSS-based VI and 67.3\% for the $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$-based VI (Fig.~\ref{fig:outputs}C). The simulated median FFR decreased monotonically with DoS (0.86, 0.85, 0.80, 0.72, and 0.60 at DoS values of 0.3, 0.4, 0.5, 0.6, and 0.7, respectively; Fig.~\ref{fig:outputs}D). In the auxiliary validation set, both PSS and $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$ increased exponentially with decreasing fibrous-cap thickness across all three fibrous-cap moduli (Fig.~\ref{fig:outputs}E).

\subsection{Clinical consistency of the vulnerability indices}
The two vulnerability indices, PSS and $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$, were checked against the five clinically recognized high-risk plaque features listed in Table~\ref{tab:consistency}. For each feature, the sign of the Spearman correlation between the representative model quantity and each VI was compared with the expected high-risk direction (Fig.~\ref{fig:consistency}).

Both VIs showed the expected correlation signs for the soft-plaque ($E_\mathrm{lipid}$), less-calcified ($f_\mathrm{calc}$), and large-lipid-arc ($\theta_\mathrm{lipid}$) features. The two indices differed for plaque burden ($PB$) and functional gradient ($\Delta\mathrm{FFR}$). $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$ was positively correlated with both, matching the expected high-risk direction, whereas PSS showed the opposite sign for both. The expected correlation signs and the consistency of each VI are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:consistency}. Overall, $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$ was consistent with all five high-risk features, whereas PSS was consistent with three of the five.

