\subsection{Sensitivity analysis results}
For the two selected vulnerability indices, PSS and $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$, GPR surrogate models were constructed to approximate the relationship between the input parameters and each VI for each plaque phenotype (LAP and CP). The numbers of valid input--output pairs were 784 for LAP and 756 for CP. Hold-out validation (80/20 split) showed high surrogate accuracy, with $R^2$ values of 0.94 and 0.94 for PSS and $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$ in LAP, and 0.97 and 0.96 for PSS and $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$ in CP, respectively.

Saltelli sampling was performed using the trained GPR surrogate models to compute the first-order Sobol index ($S_1$) and total-order Sobol index ($S_T$). At the individual-variable level, $E_\mathrm{vessel}$, $E_\mathrm{FC}$, and $P_\mathrm{sys}$ were the dominant contributors for both VIs (Fig.~\ref{fig:sobol}A). For LAP, $E_\mathrm{vessel}$ showed the largest total-order index for both indices ($S_T = 0.372$ for PSS and $S_T = 0.380$ for $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$), followed by $E_\mathrm{FC}$ ($S_T = 0.247$ and $0.266$) and $P_\mathrm{sys}$ ($S_T = 0.217$ and $0.169$). For CP, $E_\mathrm{FC}$ was the leading contributor for both indices ($S_T = 0.303$ for PSS and $S_T = 0.316$ for $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$); for PSS it was followed by $P_\mathrm{sys}$ ($S_T = 0.277$) and $E_\mathrm{vessel}$ ($S_T = 0.231$), and for $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$ by $E_\mathrm{vessel}$ ($S_T = 0.235$) and $P_\mathrm{sys}$ ($S_T = 0.221$).

These results indicated that VI variability was primarily governed by $E_\mathrm{vessel}$, $E_\mathrm{FC}$, and $P_\mathrm{sys}$. In contrast, the morphological variables showed relatively low individual effects, with $S_1$ values generally below 0.08. However, several morphological variables exhibited $S_T$ values larger than their corresponding $S_1$ values, suggesting the presence of interaction-related contributions. For example, in LAP the lipid arc angle ($\theta_\mathrm{lipid,max}$) showed $S_1 = 0.058$ and $S_T = 0.104$ for PSS, and the degree of stenosis showed $S_1 = 0.002$ and $S_T = 0.055$ for $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$, i.e., an almost entirely interaction-driven contribution.

The same trend was observed in the group-level sensitivity results (Fig.~\ref{fig:sobol}B). The material group showed the largest total-order contribution across both phenotypes and both selected VIs ($S_T = 0.596$--$0.708$), followed by the hemodynamic group ($S_T = 0.217$--$0.310$). The morphology group showed the lowest $S_1$ values ($S_1 = 0.046$--$0.147$), while its total-order contribution was larger ($S_T = 0.126$--$0.247$), indicating that morphological factors contributed mainly through interactions rather than direct effects.

