\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
The main findings of this study can be summarized as follows. First, $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$ was consistent with all five clinically recognized high-risk features, whereas PSS was consistent with three of the five. This result shows that the pulsatile stress amplitude is the more clinically consistent index. Second, the global sensitivity analysis showed that the variability of both indices was governed predominantly by material properties ($E_\mathrm{vessel}$ and $E_\mathrm{FC}$), with systolic pressure ($P_\mathrm{sys}$) acting as a hemodynamic contributor. As a prerequisite for interpreting these findings, the simulation outputs were compared with previously reported computational and clinical evidence.

\subsection{Consistency of simulated outputs with prior clinical and computational evidence}
As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:outputs}A, the simulated PSS values ranged from 43 to 247~kPa, with a median of 101~kPa. This median fell within the range of prior OCT-based computational estimates, which reported $52 \pm 42$~kPa for non-ruptured plaques, $174 \pm 67$~kPa for ruptured plaques, and 150--230~kPa for vulnerable plaques~\cite{milzi2021pss, guo2026pss}, and the simulated distribution spanned the stress levels reported across these non-ruptured-to-vulnerable states. Similarly, the simulated $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$ values ranged from 14 to 79~kPa, with a median of 31~kPa, falling within the range of previously reported cyclic stress variations of 43 [34--59]~kPa in non-ruptured fibroatheromas, 55 [37--75]~kPa in ruptured fibroatheromas, and $59.3 \pm 48.2$~kPa in diseased coronary segments~\cite{costopoulos2017, huang2021dpss}.

The predicted high-risk locations followed clinically expected spatial patterns. Circumferentially, the predicted high-risk site was more frequent at the plaque shoulder than at the cap center (56.9\% and 57.5\% for PSS and $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$, respectively), consistent with pathological and \textit{in vivo} observations of preferential shoulder rupture~\cite{richardson1989, maehara2002}. Axially, high-risk sites concentrated in the mid-lesion region near the minimum lumen area (60.2\% and 67.3\%), consistent with imaging studies reporting frequent rupture near the mid-lesion segment~\cite{chung2017_LA}.

The simulated median FFR decreased monotonically with increasing DoS, from 0.86 to 0.60 as DoS increased from 0.3 to 0.7. This trend followed the expected inverse FFR--DoS relationship, remaining within the reported clinical reference band and tracking its lower edge for severe stenoses (DoS $>$ 0.6)~\cite{kristensen_ffr_ds, dai_ffr_ds, nijjer_ffr_ds, lee_ffr_ds}. The larger apparent FFR reduction in the severe range coincided with a wider spread among the reference curves in the same range (Fig.~\ref{fig:outputs}D). This pattern is consistent with prior evidence that FFR becomes increasingly sensitive to lesion geometry and flow conditions as stenosis severity increases~\cite{TODO_newcombe2021}.

In the auxiliary validation set, both PSS and $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$ increased exponentially as fibrous-cap thickness decreased across all three fibrous-cap moduli (Fig.~\ref{fig:outputs}E). This trend is consistent with prior structural analyses reporting an exponential rise in peak cap stress with decreasing cap thickness~\cite{TODO_li2006cap, TODO_akbar2022cap}, in line with the steep increase in rupture risk clinically observed for caps below ${\sim}65\,\mu\mathrm{m}$~\cite{TODO_oct_fct2022}.

Overall, these comparisons indicate that the generated dataset reproduced prior clinical and computational trends, supporting its use as the input space for the subsequent clinical-consistency check and sensitivity analysis.

\subsection{Stress amplitude versus peak stress as a vulnerability index}
Among the two vulnerability indices, $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$ was consistent with all five high-risk features, whereas PSS was not. This result indicates that the pulsatile stress amplitude ($\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$) is a more clinically consistent index than the peak systolic stress (PSS). This finding is consistent with the view that plaque rupture may involve fatigue-type damage accumulation under repeated cardiac loading~\cite{bank2000}. In an \textit{in vivo} VH-IVUS cohort, Costopoulos et al.~\cite{costopoulos2017} found that ruptured fibroatheromas exhibited a larger cyclic variation in PSS than non-ruptured ones, supporting the relevance of stress amplitude to plaque fatigue. This supports assessing vulnerability from the cyclic stress amplitude rather than from a single peak-stress snapshot, which requires pulsatile simulation rather than static analysis.

This result may be explained by the geometric dependence of peak stress. In the present geometric parameterization, plaque burden and $\Delta\mathrm{FFR}$ both reflect increasing stenotic severity, because greater $DoS$ reduces $FFR$ and therefore increases $\Delta\mathrm{FFR}$~\cite{pijls1996ffr, kristensen_ffr_ds}. As a peak systolic quantity, PSS reflects the combined effects of multiple local geometric factors, including lumen radius, lumen area, and fibrous-cap thickness~\cite{teng2014, TODO_more}. Therefore, it cannot be interpreted solely as a function of plaque burden. Consistent with this interpretation, prior work reported that PSS does not necessarily increase with plaque burden and may even correlate negatively with it~\cite{teng2014, brown2016}.

The lack of a positive PSS--plaque-burden relationship may be explained by the Laplace relation $\sigma_\theta \sim P r / t$. Under otherwise similar pressure and wall-thickness conditions, a smaller lumen radius reduces the predicted circumferential stress. This may explain why PSS can underrepresent some high-burden lesions that are clinically regarded as vulnerable~\cite{teng2014, TODO_burden_stress}. By contrast, $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$ captures the systole-to-diastole stress amplitude rather than the absolute stress at a single instant, and may therefore provide a more appropriate stress metric for the proposed vulnerability-index framework.

\subsection{Material-dominated sensitivity hierarchy}
At the individual-parameter level, the sensitivity hierarchy was mainly characterized by material stiffness and pressure loading. Across both vulnerability indices, $E_\mathrm{vessel}$, $E_\mathrm{FC}$, and $P_\mathrm{sys}$ were consistently identified as the major contributors. Pressure loading directly affects the wall stress field, whereas the material parameters determine how this loading is transmitted and concentrated within the plaque structure.

When the parameters were grouped by physical category, the material group showed the largest contribution, followed by the hemodynamic group and then the morphological group. Thus, although pressure-related variables such as $P_\mathrm{sys}$ and $\Delta P$ were important contributors, the overall sensitivity structure was primarily material-dominated. In contrast, the morphology group showed relatively small first-order effects but larger total-order contributions, suggesting that morphological variables influenced both VIs mainly through interactions with material and hemodynamic factors rather than through independent main effects. This finding is consistent with previous computational studies showing that the stress effect of plaque morphology is strongly modulated by tissue material properties~\cite{TODO_akyildiz2011, TODO_akyildiz2016}.

\subsection{Limitations}
This study has four main limitations. First, the geometry was idealized as an axisymmetric vessel with uniform fibrous-cap thickness and no curvature, bifurcation, or asymmetric plaque morphology. Second, a linear-elastic constitutive model was adopted for the solid domain, whereas near-rupture thin caps may deform nonlinearly and tissue anisotropy may not be negligible, so extension to hyperelastic, anisotropic laws such as the Holzapfel--Gasser--Ogden model~\cite{hgo} remains future work. Third, the fibrous-cap strength was treated as a constant because patient-specific strength cannot currently be measured \textit{in vivo}; micro-indentation, atomic force microscopy, or image-based stiffness--strength models could provide sample-specific strength estimates in future work. Fourth, the VI is a surrogate validated only for directional consistency with established high-risk features, not against rupture events or patient outcomes. Therefore, comparison with prospective imaging cohorts or longitudinal data is an essential next step before clinical application.

%% ============================================================
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
This study aimed to identify which of the morphological, material, and hemodynamic factor groups most strongly governs the variability of coronary plaque rupture risk. To overcome the high computational cost of fully coupled pulsatile FSI, which has previously limited the scale of parametric analyses, a cost-effective snapshot-based FSI formulation was used as the forward solver. The pulsatile response was computed once using a 1D reduced-order model, and the representative stress field was reconstructed using 3D static structural analysis.

Using this forward solver, large input--output datasets were generated for the low-attenuation (LAP) and calcified (CP) plaque phenotypes. Two stress-based vulnerability indices, PSS and $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$, were evaluated for their directional consistency with clinically established high-risk plaque features. $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$ was consistent with all five features, whereas PSS was consistent with three of the five. This result indicates that plaque rupture vulnerability is more consistently captured by the pulsatile stress amplitude than by the peak systolic stress alone, supporting the use of pulsatile rather than static analysis.

Gaussian process regression surrogates were then used to perform Sobol variance-based global sensitivity analysis at both the individual-variable and factor-group levels. Across both plaque phenotypes and both indices, $E_\mathrm{vessel}$, $E_\mathrm{FC}$, and $P_\mathrm{sys}$ were the dominant individual contributors. At the group level, the material group showed the largest total-order contribution ($S_T = 0.596$--$0.708$), followed by the hemodynamic group, whereas the morphological group contributed mainly through interactions rather than direct effects. These results indicate that the variability of coronary plaque rupture risk is governed primarily by material and hemodynamic factors rather than by morphology alone.

Together, these findings demonstrate that the proposed framework enables an efficient, pulsatility-aware, and mechanically interpretable analysis of the factors governing coronary plaque rupture-risk variability. By quantifying the dominant determinants of this variability, the framework provides a mechanistic basis for prioritizing the quantities that warrant accurate characterization in future patient-specific assessment. Future work should extend the framework to patient-specific geometries, nonlinear and anisotropic constitutive models, and prospective clinical cohorts for direct validation against rupture events or longitudinal outcomes.

